# .gitleaksignore — stop-words for gitleaks scan (see .github/workflows/secret-scan.yml).
#
# Each non-comment line is a "stop word" that suppresses a finding whose file
# path matches it. Only entries below are intentional.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LPR-002 / DR-001 — historical Resend live-key leak in test-resend.js
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# test-resend.js contained a live Resend API key (`re_...`) in git history
# from the introduction of the file through commit f181cec (parent of b11f9f5),
# which scrubbed it to a `REDACTED_USE_RESEND_API_KEY_ENV` placeholder. The
# placeholder commit did NOT rewrite history, so the original key is still in
# the object store and every clone/fork/cached checkout.
#
# This allowlist exists ONLY so the scanner can keep detecting NEW leaks
# rather than failing forever on a historical one that has already been
# triaged and is owned under LPR-002.
#
# The exposed key MUST still be rotated at the Resend dashboard. Removing this
# allowlist line after history is purged (git filter-repo + coordinated
# force-push) is part of closing LPR-002 — not optional.
#
# Scope: this entry ignores ALL findings in the file `test-resend.js` across
# every commit. A regression that puts a NEW secret into a recreated
# `test-resend.js` would still be caught by the HEAD-only `credential-marker`
# job in the secret-scan workflow — defense-in-depth, not a free pass.
test-resend.js
